<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- ================================================================
     ADAPTIVE LAUNCHER ICON - ROUND 26, v0.9.11 (item 123)
     Variant "Ollidar llama" - owner-supplied artwork, approved in full.

     This file is wiring only. It names three layers and contains no art,
     no colours and no geometry, so a future icon revision touches the
     layer files and NOT this one.

     THE PIPELINE
       background  @color/ic_launcher_background   values/ic_launcher_background.xml (cream #F2F1EC)
       foreground  @mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground  llama + hat + orange lidar fan
       monochrome  @mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome  the same line art as an alpha
                                                   silhouette (Android 13+ themed icons)

     WHY RASTER, WHEN ROUND 22's ICON WAS VECTOR
     The artwork is a hand-drawn illustration, not a geometric mark: the
     fleece is ~40 free curves and the lidar fan is ~250 individually sized
     dots. Transcribing that into VectorDrawable pathData - which is what
     round 22 did for the slice-scan A, by generating the path arithmetic in
     a script - would be a redrawing rather than a transcription, and the
     result would not be the file the owner approved. So the layers ship as
     PNG mipmaps at all five densities (108/162/216/324/432 px = 108 dp),
     generated from masters/master-flat-square-1024.png.

     THE SAFE ZONE, AND WHY THE ART IS 62 % OF THE CANVAS
     An adaptive layer is 108 dp and the launcher's mask crops it to at most
     72 dp; only a 66 dp CIRCLE is guaranteed. The artwork is full-bleed by
     design (the llama's chest runs off the bottom-left, the fan's outer dots
     reach the top-right corner), so it is scaled to 67 dp - 62 % of 108 -
     and centred. At that size a round mask clips only cream, while a
     square/squircle mask leaves a few dp of cream margin that is invisible
     because the background layer is the same cream. Scaling it larger was
     tried and measured: at 72 dp the round mask eats the outer fan dots and
     the muzzle.

     The master's own orange frame is NOT in the foreground. Cropping it out
     is deliberate - see the comment on @color/ic_launcher_background.

     HOW TO SWAP IN A NEW ICON (the one-change procedure)
       1. Put the new 1024 px square master where the generator can see it.
       2. Regenerate mipmap-*/ic_launcher_foreground.png and
          mipmap-*/ic_launcher_monochrome.png at 108/162/216/324/432 px, art
          centred at 62 % unless the new art is not full-bleed.
       3. Re-sample the plate colour into values/ic_launcher_background.xml -
          it must equal the artwork's own background exactly (seam + parallax).
       4. Move the files it replaced to *_&lt;variant&gt;.xml/.png rather than
          deleting them, matching drawable/ic_launcher_foreground_slicescan.xml.
       5. Build :app:assembleDebug and check the launcher under BOTH a round
          and a squircle mask.
     This file stays untouched.

     RETIRED, kept retrievable, referenced by nothing:
       drawable/ic_launcher_foreground_slicescan.xml  (round 22 "slice-scan A")
       drawable/ic_launcher_monochrome_slicescan.xml  (its themed-icon layer)
       drawable/ic_launcher_foreground_legacy.xml     (teal radar sweep)
       drawable/ic_launcher_background_legacy.xml     (solid #12181F vector)

     NOTE: minSdk is 29, so every supported device takes this anydpi-v26
     definition. The mipmap-*dpi buckets exist only to hold the two LAYER
     bitmaps; there is no legacy square/round PNG icon and none is needed.
     ================================================================ -->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
